WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.304
Read the failures before you change anything.

2
00:00:02.584 --> 00:00:08.109
When cases fail, the most valuable thing you can do is the least glamorous: read the outputs.

3
00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:17.093
Hamel Husain’s essay argues that looking at real outputs is where most improvement comes from, and that teams skip it because it feels slow.

4
00:00:17.373 --> 00:00:18.803
It is faster than guessing.

5
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:21.557
As you read, sort each failure into a bucket.

6
00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:39.139
A starting set that works for most prompts: format (the output didn’t parse or broke the contract), wrong answer (parsed, but the label or fact is wrong), ignored instruction (a stated rule was not followed), missing context (the model couldn’t have known; the prompt never said),

7
00:00:39.419 --> 00:00:42.811
and bad test (the check or the expected answer is wrong).

8
00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:44.179
Count the buckets.

9
00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:47.317
Then fix the biggest bucket first, with one change.

10
00:00:47.597 --> 00:00:50.307
The last bucket matters more than people expect.

11
00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:59.888
Sometimes the model’s answer is defensible and your expected label is the mistake, or the case is ambiguous and needs two acceptable answers.

12
00:01:00.168 --> 00:01:03.837
Fixing the test is legitimate, as long as you write down why.

13
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:18.752
Anthropic’s real-world prompting course (adapted here) frames this as a lifecycle: create an initial prompt, test it and identify issues, select the techniques that address those issues, implement the improvements, and iterate.

14
00:01:19.032 --> 00:01:27.928
This bucket-and-count habit is a light version of error analysis, which Studio 3 develops into a full method with model graders and dashboards.
